<%# The hold-to-confirm button's lab page.

    Two stacks, rendered by name from the engine: the default LIVELY level and
    the CALM one. Nothing here reimplements the button — the whole point of the
    specs is the browser program inside studio/_hold_button and the computed
    styles inside engine-motion.css, so a lab page that hand-rolled either would
    grade itself.

    The x-data wrapper is what a real caller supplies: the button evaluates its
    guard / on_success expressions against the surrounding Alpine scope, and
    `submitting` is the flag it refuses to start a second hold on. `duration` is
    short so a spec can hold the button for its whole life without the lane
    paying two seconds per press.

    The palette is bound STATICALLY here (fizz_colors), which is the shape a host
    uses when its colours are known at render time; turf-monster's board binds
    the same slots live through fizz_bind. Six zones, three colours each, so a
    spec can assert a zone resolves to ONE colour rather than to eighteen. %>
<main class="p-8" x-data="{ submitting: false }">
  <p data-test="lab-body">hold button lab</p>

  <div style="max-width: 20rem">
    <%= render "studio/hold_button",
          hold_id: "lively",
          duration: 600,
          default_text: "Hold to Confirm",
          hold_text: "Almost there…",
          success_text: "Confirmed!",
          fizz_colors: %w[#8b5cf6 #4c1d95 #a78bfa
                          #22d3ee #0e7490 #67e8f9
                          #f59e0b #78350f #fbbf24
                          #ef4444 #7f1d1d #fca5a5
                          #10b981 #064e3b #6ee7b7
                          #ec4899 #831843 #f9a8d4] %>
  </div>

  <div style="max-width: 20rem; margin-top: 3rem">
    <%= render "studio/hold_button",
          hold_id: "calm",
          fizz_level: :calm,
          duration: 600,
          default_text: "Hold to Confirm" %>
  </div>

  <%# A PHONE-SHAPED CARD that clips, the frame the portal exists for: an
      overflow-hidden, rounded box with the button near its top edge. "clipped"
      is the default stack, whose bubbles the card cuts off; "escaping" opts in
      to fizz_portal and should rise past the edge. %>
  <div style="display: flex; gap: 3rem; margin-top: 6rem">
    <div data-test="phone-clipped" style="width: 16rem; height: 8rem; overflow: hidden; border-radius: 2rem; background: #111; padding: 1.25rem 1rem; position: relative; z-index: 5">
      <%= render "studio/hold_button", hold_id: "clipped", duration: 600, default_text: "Hold to Confirm" %>
    </div>
    <div data-test="phone-escaping" style="width: 16rem; height: 8rem; overflow: hidden; border-radius: 2rem; background: #111; padding: 1.25rem 1rem; position: relative; z-index: 5">
      <%= render "studio/hold_button", hold_id: "escaping", duration: 600, default_text: "Hold to Confirm",
                 fizz_colors: %w[#ff0000], fizz_portal: true %>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%# THE TIMERS. Each hook writes when it ran, relative to the press, so a
      spec can read the order and the gaps: on_hold_start at once, validate at
      validate_at, the early action at early_action_at, and on_success never,
      because an early action that fires cancels the hold's own timer. %>
  <div style="max-width: 20rem; margin-top: 3rem"
       x-data="{ submitting: false, log: [], at(name) { this.log.push([name, Math.round(performance.now() - window.__holdT0)]); window.__holdLog = this.log; } }">
    <%= render "studio/hold_button",
          hold_id: "timed",
          duration: 600,
          default_text: "Hold to Confirm",
          on_hold_start: "window.__holdT0 = performance.now(); d.log.splice(0); d.at('start')",
          validate: "(d.at('validate'), true)",
          validate_at: 150,
          early_action: "d.at('early')",
          early_action_at: 400,
          on_success: "d.at('success')" %>
  </div>
</main>
